        "id": 66185,
        "name": "razor831",
        "username": "razor831",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/r/8c91f0/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-06-22T19:57:20.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Huh, seen no straight answer on Hoefle yet.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>My German IS a little rusty, but if I'm not mistaken, the e at the end wouldn't be silent.&nbsp; The oe is a stand-in for <b>ö</b>, which isn't QUITE pronounced like \"er,\" but that's a good approximation for native English speakers.&nbsp; To actually get the right sound, you need to purse your lips like you're going to make an \"oo\" sound, but then make an \"oh\" sound through that pursing.&nbsp; The -le at the end would be \"luh.\"</p>",
        "post_number": 28,
